---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const LAST_UPDATED = '2026-10-17';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How singletenant.ai gathers vendor data, what 'verified' means, the inclusion criteria, and how to submit a correction."
>
  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 md:py-16">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Methodology</p>
      <h1 class="m-0 mb-2" style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        How this data is made
      </h1>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs mb-10" style="color: var(--color-mute);">
        Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}
      </p>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Primary sources only</h2>
        <p>
          Every fact in the vendor database is taken from a primary source:
          the vendor's own documentation, trust centre, legal terms, or
          pricing pages, read at the time of the check. Third-party
          writeups, forum threads, and press coverage are not sources.
          Nothing is filled in from general knowledge or assumption.
        </p>

        <h2>What "verified" means</h2>
        <p>
          A populated fact always carries two things: the URL of the
          primary source it was checked against, and the date of that
          check. "Verified" means the linked page supported the claim on
          that date, not that the claim is true forever. Dates are shown
          per section so staleness is visible rather than hidden.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where a fact could not be confirmed from a primary source, the
          profile shows <strong>"Not verified"</strong>. An honest gap is
          more useful to a procurement decision than a guess.
        </p>

        <h2>Inclusion criteria</h2>
        <p>
          The database covers vendors offering single-tenant LLM hosting
          (inference infrastructure where the customer's workload runs on
          capacity not shared with other customers) in five categories:
          dedicated GPU/inference clouds, hyperscaler dedicated capacity,
          managed self-host platforms, EU sovereign clouds, and UK sovereign
          clouds. A vendor
          qualifies if it publicly documents a dedicated or single-tenant
          deployment option. Commercial relationships play no part in
          inclusion; see the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a>.
          The rules on sourcing, verification, corrections, and conflicts of
          interest are set out in the
          <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Region buckets</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>Regions</strong> column records coarse geographies where a
          vendor documents capacity, not every named data-centre location. A
          bucket is added only where the vendor's own material names that
          geography; where a source does not distinguish a geography, it is not
          inferred. The buckets are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>US</strong>: the United States.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>UK</strong>: the United Kingdom, tracked separately from the
            EU/EEA. After Brexit the UK is a distinct jurisdiction for data
            purposes, so a vendor that names a UK location is recorded as
            <code>uk</code>, not folded into Europe.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Europe</strong>: the EU/EEA, <strong>excluding the UK</strong>.
            A vendor serving both is recorded as both <code>europe</code> and
            <code>uk</code>. This bucket narrowed when UK was split out; a vendor
            still showing only Europe has no UK location named in its sources.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Asia-Pacific</strong>: the APAC region.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Global</strong>: the vendor documents worldwide or
            multi-region availability without a per-region breakdown at review.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Regions describe where compute can run. Whether a vendor contractually
          guarantees data stays in a chosen region is a separate, per-vendor
          verified fact in the Data residency column, and jurisdiction (the law
          a provider answers to) is separate again. See the
          <a href="/blog/jurisdiction-eu-region-not-sovereign/">jurisdiction
          analysis</a> for why an EU or UK region does not settle the
          jurisdiction question.
        </p>

        <h2>Update cadence</h2>
        <p>
          The database is reviewed manually. There is no automated
          collection. Every addition, update, and correction is a dated
          entry in the public <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, so you
          can see exactly what changed and when.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          If a fact on this site is wrong or has gone stale, email
          <a href="mailto:editor@singletenant.ai">editor@singletenant.ai</a>
          with the page, the claim, and (if you have it) a link to the
          primary source that contradicts it. We re-check the claim against
          the vendor's published material; if it fails, the data is
          corrected, the verified date is updated, and the correction is
          recorded in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, as set out in
          the <a href="/editorial-policy/#corrections">editorial policy</a>.
          Vendors are
          welcome to submit corrections about their own profiles on the
          same terms: a correction needs a primary source, whoever sends
          it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
